[[0,1297092,0,2,0,2,"2016-09-19T22:53:43","Mixplate","",[],"","Cable lobby tries to make you forget that it represents cable companies","http://arstechnica.com/information-technology/2016/09/biggest-cable-lobby-drops-the-word-cable-from-its-name/","whatever"],[1,6360855,0,2,0,2,"2016-09-19T23:20:04","derram","",[],"<p><a href=\"https://archive.is/Rb3s2\">https://archive.is/Rb3s2</a> : \r</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Cable lobby tries to make you forget that it represents cable companies | Ars Technica </p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>'In 2001, Cable Television was dropped from the name and replaced with Cable &amp; Telecommunications. ' </p>\n<p>'The National Cable &amp; Telecommunications Association (NCTA) will henceforth be called NCTA-The Internet &amp; Television Association. ' </p>\n<p>'The CTIA—previously known as the Cellular Telecommunications Industry Association and then the Cellular Telecommunications &amp; Internet Association—is now just &quot;CTIA-The Wireless Association.&quot;' </p>\n<p>'Despite dropping the word &quot;cable,&quot; the NCTA's name change announcement makes reference to how cable companies are dominating the broadband market. ' </p>\n<p>'Last year, the NCTA also changed the name of its annual conference from &quot;The Cable Show&quot; to &quot;INTX: The Internet &amp; Television Expo.&quot;' </p>\n<p><a href=\"https://vgy.me/VozQ7Z.png\">This has been an automated message.</a></p>"]]
